---
const meta = {
  title: "SAUTI KWA UKUTA",
  subtitle: "A Gender-Sensitive Lived Narratives and Resources Hub",
  publisher: "Students For Sensible Drug Policy (SSDP International) Kenya Chapter",
  support: "The global Support.Don't Punish Campaign and Women and Harm Reduction International Network",
  published: "June 2026 — Nairobi, Kenya",
};

const stories = [
  {
    id: "the-tuesday-eviction",
    title: "THE TUESDAY EVICTION",
    label: "Campus & Institutional Violence",
    location: "University Campus, Kenya",
    narrator: "Anonymous — University Student",
    font: "'Caveat', cursive",
    pullQuote: "The intense stress of that whole situation made me drink even more alcohol just to numb the constant anxiety. I honestly don't know if I'll ever graduate. But this is my first step toward fixing things: sharing my story.",
    narrative: [
      "Housekeepers were doing a maintenance check on a random Tuesday and I was caught in my university hostel room with a couple of empty alcohol bottles. Some of the bottles were just for aesthetics — I use wine bottles as flower vessels — and two were mine, I have to admit that. I wasn't given a chance to explain myself and the bottles were also empty, so… I was summarily evicted from the room on the spot.",
      "Because I had absolutely nowhere else to go and couldn't afford a private apartment outside campus, my boyfriend offered to let me secretively stay in his room in the men's hostel. Looking back, those were the toughest days of my campus life.",
      "For the rest of the semester, my life became a terrifying game of hide-and-seek. I had to sneak in late at night and wake up incredibly early to slip out before anyone noticed. It could be a whole other bunch of trouble if I was caught at my boyfriend's room by their custodian. Every single day was a struggle; even using the shared bathrooms felt deeply unsafe and uncomfortable because I was a girl hiding in a strictly male environment. The constant paranoia and anxiety of being caught by the security guards or harassed by other guys in the hall completely shattered my peace of mind.",
      "The irony is, the intense stress of that whole situation made me drink even more alcohol just to numb the constant anxiety and forget how uncomfortable my life had become. I did this almost every day. I had my favorite spot downtown where I used to go buy relatively cheap liquor. As sad as it is to admit, I have struggled with it; as a result, I have missed classes a lot, some exams, and CATs. I honestly don't know if I'll ever graduate. But this is my first step toward fixing things: sharing my story.",
    ],
    advocacy: {
      heading: "A Health-Centered Path Forward",
      body: "A harm reduction approach recognizes that empty bottles shouldn't cost a student their education, housing, or physical safety. By offering confidential guidance and wellness checks instead of summary punishments and immediate evictions, institutions can help students manage stress safely, stay in class, and successfully reach graduation.",
    },
    support: "If you are struggling with heavy alcohol use and academic stress, we offer an empathetic peer community. We provide non-judgmental spaces to discuss substance relationships and can point you toward youth-friendly, confidential mental health professionals and support groups that can help you manage your wellness without risking your education.",
  },
  {
    id: "the-methadone-journey",
    title: "THE METHADONE JOURNEY",
    label: "Harm Reduction & Recovery",
    location: "Nairobi, Kenya",
    narrator: "Anonymous — Small Business Owner, Age 26",
    font: "'Kalam', cursive",
    pullQuote: "Methadone gave me my function back. It took away the agonizing withdrawal symptoms and also the constant craving in my brain. I gained the stability to plan — and look at me now. I'm running my own business.",
    narrative: [
      "I am twenty-six years old, and right now, I run a small, busy stall in Nairobi selling smocha. The best smocha in my mtaa by the way. Smocha is basically chapati wrapped around smoky sausage with kachumbari. I go the extra mile with my smocha — I add ketchup and avocado. That's why mine is the best. It is my honest hustle, and I love it.",
      "But my journey didn't start last year; it started at the Coast, kule Pwani. I studied my bachelor's at a university in Coast and also attempted to start my life after school there — if you see me here now you know it was indeed an attempt. I first learned how to use heroin when I was in my third year at the university. I won't lie, it had a tight grip on me.",
      "I came back to Nairobi at 24, in search of employment opportunities, as I wanted to start life afresh. Adulthood had started settling in — responsibility and everything. Getting employment in Nairobi is as hard as the coast, so I landed casual jobs, but severe withdrawal would kick in. The body aches, the sweating, the intense shaking, and the overwhelming sickness made it physically impossible to keep the casual work. When the cravings and pain would get too heavy I would relapse, then try to stop, then relapse again. It became a cycle of trying to work, falling sick from withdrawal, and relapsing to survive.",
      "Everything changed when I discovered the harm reduction clinic in Ngara that offers Medically Assisted Therapy. Starting methadone was the turning point of my life. It took away the agonizing withdrawal symptoms and also the constant craving in my brain. I gained the stability to plan, and look at me now. I'm running my own business. I can say Methadone gave me my function back. It has been a journey for the books.",
    ],
    advocacy: {
      heading: "What You Need to Know About Methadone & Harm Reduction",
      body: "Opioid Dependence Treatment (ODT) through Medically Assisted Therapy (MAT) like the methadone program in Ngara is an evidence-based healthcare solution, not a moral failure. Methadone replaces illicit opioids under medical supervision, stopping withdrawal and cravings without producing a \"street high.\" This allows individuals to stabilize their lives, return to work, and improve their health. Harm reduction recognizes that relapse is often a direct result of untreated physical withdrawal; instead of punishing people for struggling, it provides them with the medical support needed to reclaim their economic independence and dignity.",
    },
    support: "If you or a loved one are struggling with opioid use, heavy withdrawal, or finding it hard to stabilize your daily hustle, you do not have to fight it alone. We can connect you safely and confidentially to friendly, non-judgmental harm reduction clinics and MAT programs. We believe in healthcare, support, and tools that empower you to build your life on your own terms. Your past does not define your future; let us walk with you.",
  },
  {
    id: "kosa-sio-ruhusa",
    title: "KOSA SIO RUHUSA",
    label: "Campus & Institutional Violence",
    location: "University Campus, Kenya",
    narrator: "Anonymous — Final-Year University Student",
    font: "'Shadows Into Light', cursive",
    pullQuote: "The university's zero-tolerance policy didn't protect me; it gave a predator complete power to blackmail and extort me because he knew I had no voice.",
    narrative: [
      "My relationship with weed is very interesting. I don't smoke it every day, but it helps me get the urge to eat food sometimes and that's what I love to use it for. When I was in my final year at the University, I used to use weed in some of the evenings just to cope with intense academic anxiety and stress. At this point, I had moved from staying with my parents to the University hostel. This was to save time spent commuting and easily access the Library. I loved our Library.",
      "One night we were coming back from eating supper and I was in the company of a few friends from class outside my hostel. While going in, I accidentally dropped my lighter at the entrance and the Custodian saw it. It was a male custodian on duty that night. He asked me to provide my student's ID, go to my room, and bring him whatever I was smoking. I didn't have cannabis at that time, but I had the scent on me and he had the lighter and my student's ID, which he swore could be used as evidence — I was going to be suspended for 1,000 academic days.",
      "Instead of reporting the incident right away, he cornered me and threatened me. He explicitly told me that if I didn't give him a sexual favor right then, he would call the security team and write a note that would be used by the Administration for my 1,000 academic day suspension. I was paralyzed with fear. Being in my final semester, I felt I had no choice and couldn't afford to be suspended, so I complied with his demand. Afterward, I was completely overwhelmed by guilt and disgust.",
      "Because of that pain, I decided after a few days to pack up and go back to my parents' house — at least I had a place I could still go to. What if I didn't have a place to go to? What choices could I make? Am I safe anywhere as a woman using drugs? The bottom line is someone in the position of Authority took advantage of my vulnerability. That kind of abuse of power is completely separate from the school rules I had violated, and should never have happened. They should have given me a chance. I made a mistake, but it did not permit him to weaponize my vulnerability against me.",
    ],
    advocacy: {
      heading: "Advocating for Health-Centered Campus Policies",
      body: "University drug policies should focus more on health-centered frameworks that balance campus rules with student well-being. By adopting an evidence-based prevention and harm reduction approach, institutions can create supportive environments where students feel safe seeking help for academic stress, substance use, or harassment without fearing isolation or exploitation.",
    },
    support: "If you are a student facing institutional intimidation, blackmail, or harassment from staff, you do not have to navigate the fear alone. SSDP Kenya is a safe space where you can share your experience in total confidence. While we are not a legal defense team, we can safely listen to you, stand with you as peers, and guide you toward trusted external legal aid and specialized gender-based violence response organizations.",
  },
  {
    id: "the-cost-of-being-seen",
    title: "THE COST OF BEING SEEN",
    label: "Profiling & Gender-Based Violence",
    location: "Westlands, Nairobi, Kenya",
    narrator: "Anonymous — Gender-Diverse Person (They/Them)",
    font: "'Patrick Hand', cursive",
    pullQuote: "The danger for us doesn't come from the substances; it comes from the system. I was completely sober and calm, but the stigma around who I am and what was in my bag gave people in power a license to abuse me.",
    narrative: [
      "I am a gender-diverse person; my personal pronouns are They/Them. I get bashed about my pronouns a lot, but I really don't care. I use drugs occasionally. I am incredibly careful about it; I eat healthy, drink a lot of water, I exercise (recently enrolled in a gym), I take care of my mental health, and also recently enrolled for a new course. I don't have an addiction and I don't use substances to escape.",
      "For me and my friends, using prescription downers like Rohypnol or Diazepam is just a choice we make once in a while during weekends to unwind, relax, and connect at parties where we can fully be ourselves, dress how we want, and use our 'They/Them' pronouns safely.",
      "The danger for us doesn't come from the substances; it comes from the system. One weekend, a few friends and I were leaving a venue in Westlands when we were randomly stopped and searched by local security guards right outside the premises. I had two blisters of Diazepam in my bag, just standard medication. But because of how I dress and express my gender, the guards immediately weaponized it. I believe I was an easy target because of how I express myself and my gender, because I was completely sober and calm.",
      "We were intimidated, profiled, and mockingly hurled slurs at my gender expression, and they threatened to call the police to lock us up for drug possession. As a person who identifies as They/Them, the threat of being thrown into a police cell is a threat of extreme physical and sexual violence. You know we had to pay them to let us leave — hilarious. The entire experience was violating and deeply unfair. I did nothing wrong, I wasn't harming anyone, and I was very calm, but the stigma around who I am and what was in my bag gave people in power a license to abuse me.",
    ],
    advocacy: {
      heading: "How Harm Reduction & Policy Reform Changes the Narrative",
      body: "Drug policy reform must acknowledge that substance use exists on a wide spectrum, and occasional use does not justify violence, extortion, or profiling. When policies treat all drug use as an automatic criminal act, it creates a dangerous environment where security personnel and authority figures can easily exploit and abuse women and gender-diverse individuals. True harm reduction means dismantling criminalization and stigma so that people who choose to use substances can do so safely, without fearing for their physical security or human rights.",
    },
    support: "While SSDP Kenya cannot intervene during police or security encounters, we provide a strongly protective, intersectional peer community where your identity is completely validated. We can share peer-tested safety strategies and connect you to local human rights and legal advocacy networks dedicated to protecting gender-diverse youth.",
  },
  {
    id: "rangi-za-upinzani",
    title: "RANGI ZA UPINZANI",
    label: "Profiling & Residential Harassment",
    location: "Nairobi, Kenya",
    narrator: "Anonymous — Visual Artist & Fine Arts Student",
    font: "'Architects Daughter', cursive",
    pullQuote: "I won't let his profiling push me out of a space I love. My studio remains a place for creation, and we are going to keep making art, regardless of his unnecessary checks at the gate.",
    narrative: [
      "I have been drawing and painting since I was a little girl; I strongly believe I was born with a brush in hand. I am a visual artist, and I also study fine arts at the University. My apartment is also my studio, and this is my sanctuary. I love, love the smell of turpentine — my whole apartment smells like it. My canvas comes to life here, and my soul feels completely free. I use my art to speak of body positivity and also the beauty of our community. My artist friends also come to my studio and we create beautiful pieces. I also identify as a WUD (Woman Using Drugs).",
      "We look like creatives — at least I look like a creative person. With our dreadlocks, you know, how we dress, and we also tend to work unconventional hours. The security guard at our place has a massive problem with all this. He is convinced that my studio is a 'drug den.' He has turned his suspicion into a tool for daily harassment.",
      "Every single time my friends come to visit or collaborate on a piece, he intercepts them at the gate. He forces them to open their bags, thoroughly digging through their sketchbooks, paintbrushes, and personal belongings under the guise of 'security checks.' This is something not done to any other visitors. It is humiliating, exhausting, and a complete violation of our privacy.",
      "I have learned to stay completely calm, document his behavior when necessary, and remind myself of my rights as a rent-paying tenant. I won't let his profiling push me out of a space I love. My studio remains a place for creation, and we are going to keep making art, regardless of his unnecessary checks at the gate.",
    ],
    advocacy: {
      heading: "De-Criminalizing the Creative Identity & Promoting Autonomy",
      body: "When policies and societal mindsets criminalize substance use, it creates a culture of surveillance where everyday citizens — like caretakers or security guards — feel entitled to police, harass, and profile young people based entirely on their aesthetics. Harm reduction frameworks emphasize moving away from punitive neighborhood surveillance and toward health literacy and individual autonomy, ensuring that adult choices in private spaces are respected and decoupled from targeted systemic bullying.",
    },
    support: "Dealing with an invasive caretaker, hostile estate profiling, or threats to your peace of mind can be incredibly isolating. You do not have to face this daily bullying in silence. SSDP Kenya offers a strong, supportive network of fellow creatives and peers who understand exactly what you are going through. We provide safe, confidential spaces where you can vent, share practical tips on handling difficult estate dynamics, and access peer-led harm reduction education to protect your peace and validate your space.",
  },
];

const consent = "In strict alignment with global harm reduction documentation standards and ethical media procedures, all campaign imagery associated with these narratives utilizes alternative, non-identifying photography. No facial features, specific tracking locations, or identifiable landmarks are published. Every piece of documentation prioritizes the absolute safety, digital privacy, and opt-out imperatives of our community participants.";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{meta.title} | {meta.subtitle}</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A gender-sensitive lived narratives and resources hub centering the real voices and resilience of young women, gender-diverse people, and students in Kenya who use drugs." />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400;1,700&family=Source+Serif+4:ital,opsz,wght@0,8..60,400;0,8..60,500;0,8..60,600;1,8..60,400;1,8..60,500&family=Caveat:wght@400;600&family=Kalam:wght@300;400&family=Shadows+Into+Light&family=Patrick+Hand&family=Architects+Daughter&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
  </head>

  <body>

    <!-- Top bar -->
    <div class="top-bar">
      <span>{meta.publisher}</span>
      <span class="separator">·</span>
      <span>{meta.published}</span>
    </div>

    <main>

      <!-- Hero -->
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-inner">
          <p class="kicker">Lived Narratives &amp; Resources Hub</p>
          <h1>
            <span class="swahili">Sauti Kwa Ukuta</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="subtitle">{meta.subtitle}</p>
          <p class="intro">
            This is a completely safe, anonymous, and judgment-free space dedicated to centering
            the real voices, challenges, and immense resilience of young women, gender-diverse
            people, youth, and students in higher learning institutions in Kenya who use drugs.
          </p>
          <p class="mission-line">
            Punitive policies, harsh institutional rules, and societal shame do not heal our communities;
            they force women into hiding, cut them off from life-saving healthcare, and leave them
            vulnerable to violence and exploitation. <strong>Every woman deserves health, safety, dignity, and human rights.</strong>
          </p>
          <p class="disclaimer">
            SSDP Kenya Chapter does not encourage, promote, or endorse the use or possession of illegal substances.
            Our objective is strictly humanitarian and analytical: we document these lived experiences to expose
            how systemic stigma, punitive overreach, and a lack of health-centered policies directly fuel
            gender-based violence, extortion, and the violation of basic human rights.
          </p>
          <a href="#stories" class="cta">Read the stories</a>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-ornament" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="circle c1"></div>
          <div class="circle c2"></div>
          <div class="line-v"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Consent notice -->
      <div class="consent-band">
        <span class="consent-icon">&#9679;</span>
        <p>{consent}</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Stories -->
      <section id="stories" class="stories-section">
        <div class="section-label">Our Stories</div>

        {stories.map((story, index) => (
          <article class="story-block" id={story.id}>

            <div class="story-header">
              <div class="story-number">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
              <span class="story-label">{story.label}</span>
              <h2 class="story-title">{story.title}</h2>
              <div class="story-meta">
                <span>{story.narrator}</span>
                <span class="dot">·</span>
                <span>{story.location}</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="story-body">
              <blockquote class="pull-quote">
                <span class="open-quote">"</span>
                {story.pullQuote}
                <span class="close-quote">"</span>
              </blockquote>

              <div class="narrative-text" style={`font-family: ${story.font};`}>
                {story.narrative.map((para) => (
                  <p>{para}</p>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="advocacy-block">
              <div class="advocacy-inner">
                <h3>{story.advocacy.heading}</h3>
                <p>{story.advocacy.body}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="support-note">
                <span class="support-icon">❧</span>
                <p>{story.support}</p>
              </div>
            </div>

          </article>
        ))}
      </section>

      <!-- Reach Out -->
      <section class="reach-out-section">
        <div class="reach-out-inner">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">Reach Out Safely</div>
          <p class="reach-out-intro">
            If you are a young woman, student, or gender-diverse person experiencing profiling,
            institutional harassment, or looking for safe harm reduction information, you can
            connect with us completely confidentially.
          </p>
          <div class="reach-out-channels">
            <div class="channel">
              <span class="channel-label">Email</span>
              <span class="channel-value">—</span>
            </div>
            <div class="channel">
              <span class="channel-label">Signal</span>
              <span class="channel-value">—</span>
            </div>
            <div class="channel">
              <span class="channel-label">Follow our Advocacy</span>
              <span class="channel-value">—</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="reach-out-note">All communications are kept strictly confidential and anonymous.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Supported by -->
      <section class="support-section">
        <p class="support-label">Supported by</p>
        <p class="support-names">{meta.support}</p>
      </section>

    </main>

    <footer>
      <p>{meta.title} &nbsp;·&nbsp; {meta.published}</p>
      <p>You are not alone, and your voice matters.</p>
    </footer>

  </body>
</html>

<style>
  *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

  :root {
    --cream:    #f5efe6;
    --paper:    #faf7f2;
    --ink:      #1c150e;
    --ink-soft: #3d3328;
    --muted:    #7a6a58;
    --rule:     #d9cfc4;
    --terracot: #8b3a2a;
    --rust:     #b5492f;
    --warm:     #c9825a;
    --highlight:#fff4e0;
  }

  html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }

  body {
    font-family: 'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;
    background: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  /* ── TOP BAR ─────────────────────────────────── */
  .top-bar {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: #c8bfb4;
    font-family: 'Source Serif 4', serif;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.55rem 0;
    text-align: center;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .separator { opacity: 0.4; }

  /* ── MAIN ────────────────────────────────────── */
  main { width: min(860px, 92%); margin: 0 auto; }

  /* ── HERO ────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 90vh;
    display: grid;
    align-content: center;
    padding: 7rem 0 5rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .hero-inner { position: relative; z-index: 2; }

  .hero-ornament {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0; right: -6rem;
    width: 420px; height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .circle {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .c1 { width: 340px; height: 340px; top: 8%; right: 20px; }
  .c2 { width: 180px; height: 180px; top: 18%; right: 100px; background: rgba(139,58,42,0.04); border: none; }

  .line-v {
    position: absolute;
    right: 140px; top: 0; bottom: 0;
    width: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, var(--rule) 30%, var(--rule) 70%, transparent);
  }

  .kicker {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--terracot);
    font-weight: 400;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-family: 'Playfair Display', 'Georgia', serif;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 0.92;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .swahili {
    display: block;
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 7.5rem);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .intro {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    max-width: 680px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
  }

  .mission-line {
    font-size: 1rem;
    max-width: 620px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .mission-line strong {
    color: var(--terracot);
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .disclaimer {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    max-width: 620px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }

  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.8rem 2rem;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--cream);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-family: 'Source Serif 4', serif;
    font-weight: 400;
    transition: background 0.2s;
  }

  .cta:hover { background: var(--terracot); }

  /* ── CONSENT BAND ────────────────────────────── */
  .consent-band {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--cream);
    padding: 1.4rem 2rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.2rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin: 0 calc((92vw - min(860px, 92%)) / -2);
    width: 100vw;
    position: relative;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  .consent-icon {
    color: var(--rust);
    font-size: 0.55rem;
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .consent-band p {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* ── STORIES SECTION ─────────────────────────── */
  .stories-section {
    padding: 5rem 0;
  }

  .section-label {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.32em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--terracot);
    font-weight: 400;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* ── STORY BLOCK ─────────────────────────────── */
  .story-block {
    margin-bottom: 6rem;
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .story-block:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .story-header {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .story-number {
    font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
    font-size: 5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--rule);
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: -0.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .story-label {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
